{
  "dom_id": "step-q42349",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A language class has $63$ students, $43$ of them are studying English, and $25$ of them are studying Spanish. There are $10$ students studying both languages.\n\nIf $A$ represents the event that a student is studying English, and $B$ represents the event that a student is studying Spanish, which of the following gives the correct Venn diagram for the corresponding sample space?",
  "worked_solution": "Note that there are $10$ students who study both languages. So, we draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $10$ where the circles overlap.\n\n- The number of students who *only* study English is\n\n$$\n43-10=33,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $33$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B.$\n\n- The number of students who *only* study Spanish is\n\n$$\n25-10=15,\n$$\n\nso we write the number $15$ in the part of circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A.$\n\nThere are $63$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is \n\n$$\n63-10-33-15=5.\n$$\n\nTo represent the $5$ remaining students, we write the number $5$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B.$\n\nFinally, our Venn diagram looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/3da8b95c5182be1fd4c027e5e6f2ce84274286c805afdc84891bd5aecd14add9.png)",
